{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "composite": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true,

    /*
      Emit somewhere Vite will never look.

      Without this, `tsc -b` - the first half of `npm run build` - compiles
      vite.config.ts and drops vite.config.js beside it. Vite resolves
      vite.config.js *before* vite.config.ts, so from that moment the compiled copy
      is the live config and every later edit to the .ts is silently ignored. That
      happened in the marketing repo and a stale vite.config.js was the real config
      there for months.

      `noEmit` cannot be used instead: a composite project referenced by
      tsconfig.json may not disable emit (TS6310). Redirecting outDir keeps the type
      checking and throws the output away inside node_modules, which is ignored.

      If a vite.config.js ever appears next to the .ts, that is the bug - not
      whatever you were actually debugging.
    */
    "outDir": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig-node",
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig-node/tsconfig.node.tsbuildinfo"
  },
  "include": ["vite.config.ts"]
}
